---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rail from "../components/Rail.astro";
import { THERAPIES, DELIVERY, WHO, REFERENCES, STAGE_LABELS } from "../data/therapies.mjs";
import { LAST_UPDATED, PROGRAMS } from "../data/programs.mjs";
import { SITE_URL } from "../data/site.mjs";
import a from "../styles/app.module.scss";
import th from "../styles/therapies.module.scss";

const FAMILY_LABEL = { care: "Care", compensatory: "Compensatory", "root-cause": "Root-cause" };
const CELL = {
  none: { text: "→", cls: th.symFlat, title: "essentially unchanged" },
  up: { text: "↑", cls: th.symUp, title: "improves / increases" },
  na: { text: "—", cls: th.symNa, title: "not a direct effect" },
  corrects: { text: "↓ corrects", cls: th.symFix, title: "fixes the lipid defect" },
  normalizes: { text: "✓ normalizes", cls: th.symFix, title: "fixes the lipid defect" },
  restores: { text: "✓ restores", cls: th.symFix, title: "fixes the lipid defect" },
  principle: { text: "✓ in principle", cls: th.symFix, title: "corrects in principle" }
};

// family key -> module classes (dynamic names can't be looked up by string)
const FAM_TAG = { care: th.famCare, compensatory: th.famCompensatory, "root-cause": th.famRootCause };
const CARD_MOD = { care: th.cardCare, compensatory: th.cardCompensatory, "root-cause": th.cardRootCause };
const pct = stage => Math.max(3, Math.min(100, (stage / 4) * 100));

// Funding programs relevant to each therapy approach, linked from its card.
const FUNDING = {
  "supportive-care": ["clinical-trial-readiness-for-rare-diseases-r21", "rare-diseases-clinical-research-network-namdc-collaboration"],
  "elamipretide": ["forzinity-elamipretide-eap-phase-iv-sites-and-investigator-studies", "fda-orphan-products-grants-clinical-trials-rfa-fd-25-020"],
  "nr-nad-boosting": ["umdf-research-grant-program", "bsf-research-grant-program-idea-and-development-grants"],
  "abhd18-inhibition": ["ncats-preclinical-proof-of-concept-studies-for-rare-diseases-r21", "bsf-research-grant-program-idea-and-development-grants"],
  "gene-therapy-taz-replacement": ["gene-therapy-partnering-routes-rocket-forge-andelyn-askbio", "ncats-trnd-and-bridgs-in-kind-preclinical-development", "nhlbi-catalyze-program-translational-product-development"],
  "gene-correction-base-editing": ["danaher-igi-beacon-for-crispr-cures-partnering-route"],
  "enzyme-replacement": ["bsf-research-grant-program-idea-and-development-grants"]
};
const grantBySlug = Object.fromEntries(PROGRAMS.map(g => [g.slug, g]));
for (const [t, slugs] of Object.entries(FUNDING)) {
  for (const g of slugs) if (!grantBySlug[g]) throw new Error(`FUNDING: unknown grant slug ${g} on ${t}`);
}

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "MedicalWebPage",
  name: "Therapy progress in Barth syndrome",
  url: `${SITE_URL}/therapies/`,
  about: {
    "@type": "MedicalCondition",
    name: "Barth syndrome",
    alternateName: ["BTHS", "3-methylglutaconic aciduria type II"],
    associatedAnatomy: { "@type": "AnatomicalStructure", name: "Mitochondria" }
  },
  lastReviewed: "2026-08-07"
};
---
<Base
  title="Therapy progress in Barth syndrome"
  description="Every current and emerging Barth syndrome therapy — by mechanism, by what it does to the cardiolipin defect, by delivery route, and by how close it is to real use."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class={a.shell}>
    <Rail />
    <main class={`${a.doc} ${th.page}`}>
    <p class={a.crumb}><a href="/">← Funding index</a></p>
    <h1>Therapy progress</h1>
    <p class={a.sponsor}>Every current and emerging approach — by mechanism, by what it does to the cardiolipin defect, by how it's delivered, and by how close it is to real use.</p>

    <div class={`${a.note} ${a.noteAlt} ${th.note}`}>
      <strong>There is now an approved drug.</strong> On <strong>September 19, 2025</strong> the FDA granted accelerated approval to <strong>elamipretide</strong> (brand name <strong>Forzinity</strong>, Stealth BioTherapeutics) — the first treatment ever approved for Barth syndrome, and the first approved mitochondria-targeted drug. It is approved for patients weighing <strong>at least 30 kg</strong>, with important caveats spelled out in its card below.
    </div>

    <div class={`${a.note} ${a.noteAlt2} ${th.note}`}>
      <p style="margin:0 0 8px"><strong>And the gene therapy has moved further than the literature shows.</strong> <span class={th.pillUnpub}>Unpublished</span></p>
      <p style="margin:0">At the <strong>2026 Barth Syndrome Foundation conference</strong> (July 19–26, Bonita Springs, FL), <strong>Bill Pu's lab</strong> presented an optimized vector called <strong>TAZ-02</strong> — a rebuilt version of the 2020 proof-of-concept therapy with a myotropic capsid, a codon-optimized TAZΔ5 transgene, a muscle-specific promoter, and a <strong>6.7-fold lower dose</strong>. Stated next steps: a pre-IND meeting with the FDA and large-animal toxicity testing. None of it is published — it exists as conference slides.</p>
    </div>

    <p class={th.proseP}>Every therapy here is trying to solve the same core problem: broken tafazzin (TAZ) means cardiolipin can't be remodeled, so mature cardiolipin falls, the toxic intermediate <strong>MLCL</strong> builds up, the mitochondrial energy machinery destabilizes, and ATP-hungry tissues (heart, skeletal muscle) suffer. What separates the approaches is <strong>where in that chain they intervene</strong> — which splits them into two families — plus a separate question that turns out to matter just as much: <strong>how you physically get the fix into the right cells</strong>.</p>

    <div class={th.familyGrid}>
      <div class={th.familyCard}>
        <h3 class={th.famCompHead}>● Compensatory strategies</h3>
        <p>Leave the broken cardiolipin as-is, but help the surviving mitochondria work better — prop up the machinery, or build more of it, to restore ATP. Treats the consequence, not the cause. Faster to develop; symptomatic. <em>This is where today's only approved drug sits.</em></p>
      </div>
      <div class={th.familyCard}>
        <h3 class={th.famRootHead}>● Root-cause strategies</h3>
        <p>Fix the lipid or the gene itself — restore functional cardiolipin (or working tafazzin) so the machinery is built correctly. Treats the cause. More curative in concept; earlier and harder to develop.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>The eight approaches side by side</h2>
    <div class={th.tableScroll}>
      <table class={th.cmp}>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Therapy</th><th>Family</th><th>How it works</th><th>Route</th><th>Cardiolipin</th><th>MLCL</th><th>ATP</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {THERAPIES.map(t => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href={`#${t.slug}`}>{t.name}</a>
                {t.badge && <span class={th.pillApproved}>{t.badge}</span>}
              </th>
              <td><span class={`${th.famTag} ${FAM_TAG[t.family]}`}>{FAMILY_LABEL[t.family]}</span></td>
              <td>{t.how}</td>
              <td>{t.route}</td>
              <td class={CELL[t.cl].cls} title={CELL[t.cl].title}>{CELL[t.cl].text}</td>
              <td class={CELL[t.mlcl].cls} title={CELL[t.mlcl].title}>{CELL[t.mlcl].text}</td>
              <td class={CELL[t.atp].cls} title={CELL[t.atp].title}>{CELL[t.atp].text}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class={th.legend}><span class={th.symUp}>↑</span> improves · <span class={th.symFlat}>→</span> essentially unchanged · <span class={th.symFix}>✓ / ↓</span> fixes the lipid defect · <span class={th.symNa}>—</span> not a direct effect</p>

    <h2>The therapies in depth</h2>
    {THERAPIES.map(t => (
      <section class={`${th.card} ${CARD_MOD[t.family]}`} id={t.slug}>
        <div class={th.cardHead}>
          <h3>{t.name}</h3>
          <span class={`${th.famTag} ${FAM_TAG[t.family]}`}>{FAMILY_LABEL[t.family]}{t.badge ? ` · ${t.badge}` : ""}</span>
        </div>
        <p class={th.tagline}>{t.tagline}</p>

        <dl class={th.fields}>
          <dt>Who leads it</dt><dd>{t.leads}</dd>
          <dt>Mechanism</dt><dd>{t.mechanism}</dd>
          {t.evidence && <><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>{t.evidence}</dd></>}
          <dt>On CL / MLCL</dt><dd>{t.onLipid}</dd>
          <dt>On ATP</dt><dd>{t.onAtp}</dd>
          {t.routeDetail && <><dt>Route</dt><dd>{t.routeDetail}</dd></>}
          {t.approval && <><dt>Approval</dt><dd>{t.approval}</dd></>}
        </dl>

        {t.taz02 && (
          <div class={th.taz02}>
            <p class={th.taz02Title}>TAZ-02 — the rebuilt vector, component by component <span class={th.pillUnpub}>Conference data, unpublished</span></p>
            <div class={th.tableScroll}>
              <table class={`${th.cmp} ${th.small}`}>
                <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Proof-of-concept (2020)</th><th>TAZ-02 (presented 2026)</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  {t.taz02.rows.map(r => (
                    <tr><th scope="row">{r[0]}</th><td>{r[1]}</td><td class={th.taz02New}>{r[2]}</td></tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <dl class={th.fields}>
              <dt>TAZ-02 results</dt><dd>{t.taz02.results}</dd>
              <dt>Next steps</dt><dd>{t.taz02.nextSteps}</dd>
            </dl>
            <p class={th.taz02Note}>{t.taz02.note}</p>
          </div>
        )}

        <p class={th.stageLabel}>Stage: {t.stageNote}</p>
        <div class={th.stageBar}><span style={`width:${pct(t.stage)}%`}></span></div>
        <div class={th.stageTicks}>{STAGE_LABELS.map(s => <span>{s}</span>)}</div>

        <p class={th.takeaway}>{t.takeaway}</p>

        {FUNDING[t.slug] && (
          <p class={th.fundingLine}>
            Funding for this work:{" "}
            {FUNDING[t.slug].map((g, i) => (
              <>{i > 0 && " · "}<a href={`/grants/${g}/`}>{grantBySlug[g].name}</a></>
            ))}
          </p>
        )}
      </section>
    ))}

    <h2>The other axis: delivery</h2>
    <p class={th.proseP}>Across this whole pipeline, <strong>the payload is usually the easy part; getting it into heart and skeletal muscle is the hard part.</strong> The four below aren't therapies — they're the vehicles the therapies ride in, and a better vehicle can rescue a payload that already works.</p>
    <div class={th.tableScroll}>
      <table class={th.cmp}>
        <thead><tr><th>Delivery vehicle</th><th>What it carries</th><th>Barth relevance</th><th>Stage</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {DELIVERY.map(d => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{d.name}</th><td>{d.carries}</td><td>{d.relevance}</td><td>{d.stage}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Who's working on what</h2>
    <p class={th.proseP}>The labs and companies behind each approach, collapsed into one scannable list.</p>
    <div class={th.tableScroll}>
      <table class={th.cmp}>
        <thead><tr><th>Therapy</th><th>People</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {WHO.map(w => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{w.therapy}</th><td>{w.people}</td><td>{w.where}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Where this leaves things</h2>
    <p class={th.proseP}>File the therapies by <strong>where they act</strong>. <strong>Compensatory</strong> approaches (elamipretide, MA-5, NR) leave the cardiolipin defect in place and raise ATP by different tricks — stabilizing the machinery or renewing the mitochondrial population. They're closer to use and lower-risk, but symptomatic, and this is the family that produced the first approved drug. <strong>Root-cause</strong> approaches (ABHD18 inhibition, gene therapy, base editing, enzyme replacement) actually correct the lipid or the gene, so cardiolipin and MLCL move back toward normal — more curative in concept, but earlier and harder, and each is gated by a delivery problem more than a payload problem. Gene therapy is the first of them to have visibly solved its delivery problem.</p>
    <p class={th.proseP}>Whether a therapy moves the MLCL:CL ratio sorts it into a family — unchanged means compensatory, corrected means root-cause — but that test alone is not enough to judge one. The Chin-lab penetratin result showed why: plain gene delivery corrected the ratio and still did not improve heart function, so fixing the lipid is <strong>necessary but not always sufficient</strong>. Assessing a therapy takes two questions: <strong>does it move MLCL:CL, and does it move a functional endpoint</strong> — ejection fraction, strain, or muscle strength? The strongest candidates do both.</p>
    <p class={th.proseP}>Where that lands today: <strong>supportive care</strong> is the foundation; <strong>elamipretide (Forzinity) is approved</strong> for patients ≥30 kg, on an accelerated pathway with an uneven evidence base behind it; <strong>MA-5</strong> may be next to reach patients, on thin Barth-specific evidence; and among root-cause approaches <strong>gene therapy has pulled clearly ahead</strong>, with an optimized vector characterized in mice and pre-IND work stated as the next step. ABHD18 remains the most mechanistically elegant approach and still has no molecule. Nothing in the root-cause column is in a human yet.</p>

    <div class={`${a.note} ${a.noteWarn} ${th.note}`}>
      This is an educational synthesis of the published science, <strong>not medical advice</strong>, and preclinical results in cells, flies, and mice do not reliably predict what helps a patient. Even the approved drug carries real nuance — an accelerated approval on a surrogate endpoint, a ≥30 kg weight limit, and daily injections. Several items here, including over-the-counter NR, are not proven for Barth. Any decision about care belongs with a metabolic specialist and cardiologist who know the specific mutation, weight, and clinical picture.
    </div>

    <h2>References</h2>
    <div class={`${a.note} ${th.note}`}>
      <strong>The capsid swap is real, but it is not in the literature.</strong> Pu's <em>published</em> gene therapy used AAV9 — the 2020 <em>Circulation Research</em> paper below — and the lab's most recent Barth <em>publication</em> (2025) is a new mouse model, not a therapy. The optimized TAZ-02 vector was presented at the 2026 BSF conference and has not been published, so a literature search alone will place the program where it stood in 2020. It should be cited as conference data, not as peer-reviewed work.
    </div>
    {REFERENCES.map(g => (
      <div class={th.refGroup}>
        <h3>{g.group}</h3>
        <ul>
          {g.items.map(i => (
            <li>{i.url ? <a href={i.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{i.text}</a> : i.text}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))}

    <p class={th.srcNote}>Synthesized from the August 2026 Barth therapy comparative review. Preclinical items are lab-stage, not available treatments. Spot something out of date? <a href="/suggest-update/">Suggest an update</a>.</p>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
